 pretty excited  about the broad utility of these  approaches  and uh you know when essentially the  pandemic took off we realized that there  was you know potential that you know  these types of assays like sherlock  could be really beneficial for  copenhagen detection so i want to tell a  little bit about you know our work from  the past year and a half continuing to  improve these chemistries and  diagnostics for uh startup v2 detection  so basically  early on  in the pandemic  uh you know  going back now january 2020 uh  essentially we were getting a lot of  inbound interest from other scientists  and folks who were  uh worried about what was going on in  asia and wanted us to potentially look  into using crispr enzymes to detect  the virus and the big problem was you  know there's obviously qpcr and other  more established nucleic acid detection  methods but they're slow and expensive  and you have other supply constraints  and so there's a big interest in sort of  portable point of care uh technologies  for doing this and as you can just  detect yourself at home in 30 minutes  and so you know  early on we actually uh through sherlock  biosciences  a company we co-founded we actually were  able to get fda authorization of a  crispr-based lab test so this is a test  that has to occur in central  laboratories it's too complex  to run  at home but you know i think it showed  the potential that christopher really  could be used for cover detection and  you know this was just a matter of  months it was repurposed for lab-based  testing we thought what could we do to  make at home or workplace testing  possible and so we actually went back to  the drawing board in january 2020 and  thought well what would it take to you  know take an off-the-shelf you know  pre-amplification chemistry like lamp  and actually have crispr enzymes work in  a single you know  reaction rather than having the multiple  steps i showed before  so we actually found thermophilic  enzymes  from the cast 12 family that actually  could work in the lamp master mix  and what we were able to do  is basically  design a workflow that was incredibly  simple  the idea was that someone could swab  themselves dip that swab in a lysis  buffer uh  that would break open the viral  particles then the nucleic acid would be  blind uh bound by magnetic beads which  you can then pull uh down via magnet  then we replace this lysis buffer with a  master mix uh this is what we call stop  covid and we just incubate at a single  temperature for 30 minutes to an hour  and then we can get real-time detection  by fluorescence or by a lateral flow  strip that we dipped in  and what was cool um  was what was cool about um  that slide or about this workflow um as  shown on this slide was that um  we could really simplify uh the workflow  so uh what we were able to do is  essentially uh take  uh you know a rapid extraction method  whereas other methods developed out  there including our previous protocol  would require purification that would  take 45 minutes and we could combine it  with a single step master mix uh whereas  all the other methods as i mentioned  before have a fluid transfer step as  shown inside the red arrow and so this  simplified format really could be done  in about 30 minutes which was really  great  um and so  uh  we took this chemistry and actually  profiled um almost 400 patient samples  and found that the uh you know test was  actually quantitative so our time to the  threshold the detection was nicely  correlated with the viral load as  measured by qpcr and so it was even  really sensitive down to high ct values  which are really low virus loads in the  35 to 40 range  um  this really this gave us a sensitivity  of about 93 in the specificity of 98  um which is uh we were pretty happy with  for kind of a simple point of care test  and you know going forward we've spent a  lot of time now really trying to get  this to work with all sorts of nasal  samples and making it as robust as  possible and you know our vision for the  future which you know we are working on  is to really  build a device and box around this  chemistry that someone should run at  home something that looks like an apple  homepod almost that you know belongs in  your kitchen or living room but that  actually would allow you to swab  yourself stick you know into a cartridge  that then reads out on this device and  so um i think it's an exciting time for  diagnostics because i think the  chemistries are getting really mature  and you know the crispr chemistry is  just the next step in making very  simplified formats that can be read out  anywhere and now that you know we're  taking the next step of really putting  this on devices we hope that you know  the smart home of the future has really  got you know systems that people can  really test themselves so they wake up  in the middle of night and sick and are  wondering you know do i have a you know  urinary tract infection or something or  is my fever uh due to you know just  or is it you know something more serious  like pneumonia or you know strep throat  or or so on and so um i do think that  futures that future is achievable and  that's what we're working on by making  these chemistry simple and easy and  workable in the point area to care um  sort of format  um  so  you know i think you know going beyond  diagnostics it's obvious as i mentioned  that there's a lot of interest in using  these enzymes in cellular formats for  knocking down rnas editing rnas changing  spicing and regulation of rnas and you  know we've done a little bit of that  with caster keen although casper team of  course has some toxicity concerns uh due  to the collateral effect um so we'll  show some of our work coming up with  cast 13 and also go into a new enzyme  called test 711 that was recently  published that has no collateral and can  be very precise um rna knock down the  modulation so i'll pass it on to  jonathan who will uh dive into a lot of  this exciting work um so thanks so much  thanks omar for covering all the  different things we can do in the  diagnostic side with these new rna  targeting and dna targeting tools  but  as many of you know a lot of the power  of these new crispr enzymes is the  capacity to integrate biology inside of  living cells  and so what can we do with rna targeting  tools well of course there's a lot of  different rna in the cell there's mrna  as well as trna and non-coding rnas so  the capacity to be able to target those  can actually unveil a lot of different  biology  so there are many different things we  could do with these tools  we've actually demonstrated a couple of  things in the potential therapeutics and  basic biology front including the  capacity to use their endogenous  nuclease capacity for rna knockdown as  well as the ability to do base editing  so by fusing different deaminase domains  to these enzymes we can actually be able  to make specific base edits  now we don't have time to cover both of  these today but we'll talk a little bit  about what we can do on the rna  knockdown front using both the cas13  enzymes as well as new enzymes we've  very recently discovered  now you could think it might be very  easy to apply caster t inside mammalian  cells and  to an extent that's correct  so we can actually take cast 13 and put  it in mammalian cells with a crispr rna  targeting a transcript here we use a  ratio metric reporter that has a  gaussian luciferase and a criprodine  luciferase by targeting one of the  luciferase here the gaussia  we can actually measure the ratio  between the two and see the extent of  knock down  and if we do this in hex 293 ft cells we  can actually see that  depending on how we actually  fuse either cytoplasmic or nuclear  localization sequences to the cast of  15a  we can actually get knockdown levels as  good as or  or better than rnai so you can see with  both guides one and guides two with  especially